# The files that define what this repository will and will not allow.
#
# Every path below either states a boundary (the commerce contract, its register, the control
# registry, the loop denylist), enforces one (the scanner, its tests, the workflows), or ships
# to production with no application code in between (vercel.json, next.config.ts). A change to
# any of them weakens or strengthens a rule, and a weakening edit looks like tidying in a diff.
#
# What this does today, stated rather than implied: it auto-requests a review from the owner
# on any pull request touching these paths. It does NOT block a merge. Requiring code-owner
# review in a repository with one human maintainer deadlocks every merge — GitHub blocks
# self-approval, and agents here act through the owner's identity. The ruleset turns
# `require_code_owner_review` on the day a second human reviewer exists; until then the
# enforceable set is required checks, strict mode, a pull request, and no bypass actors.
# See docs/runbooks/main-ruleset.md.
#
# Every pattern is root-anchored and must match a tracked file, or be listed as pending in
# src/tests/unit/codeowners.test.ts — a rule that matches nothing is either a typo or a rule
# that outlived its subject (ADR 035).

